,"la":38.708405,"lo":-9.139155,"d":1457},{"n":"Church of Santa Engrácia","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":38.715,"lo":-9.125,"d":376,"ds":"The Church of Santa Engrácia is a 17th-century monument in Lisbon, Portugal. Originally a church, it was converted into the National Pantheon , in which important Portuguese personalities are buried. It is located in the Alfama neighbourhood, close to another important Lisbon monument, the Monastery of São Vicente de Fora.","wpu":"Church of Santa Engrácia","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Exterior_of_Igreja_de_Santa_Engr%C3%A1cia_70.jpg/960px-Exterior_of_Igreja_de_Santa_Engr%C3%A1cia_70.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Fado Museum","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":38.711128,"lo":-9.127618,"d":412,"ds":"The Fado Museum is a music museum dedicated to Fado located in the Lisbon neighbourhood of Alfama. It was inaugurated on 25 September 1998.","wpu":"Fado Museum","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d0/Museu_do_Fado_Lisbon_Portugal_01.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Palácio Belmonte","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":38.71244,"lo":-9.13167,"d":753,"ds":"Palácio Belmonte (aka Palácio dos Condes de Belmonte) is a palace located at the Pátio de D. Fradique in Lisbon, Portugal.","wpu":"Palácio Belmonte","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/42/Pal%C3%A1cio_Belmonte_-_Lisboa_-_Portugal_%2829899100698%29.jpg/960px-Pal%C3%A1cio_Belmonte_-_Lisboa_-_Portugal_%2829899100698%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Graça Convent","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":38.716988,"lo":-9.13087,"d":879,"ds":"Graça Convent (Portuguese: Convento da Graça) is one of the oldest convents in Lisbon. It is located in Largo da Graça, in the parish of São Vicente, on Lisbon's highest hill. It faces a belvedere overlooking the city and the Tagus River.","wpu":"Graça Convent","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Igreja_e_convento_da_Gra%C3%A7a_I.jpg/960px-Igreja_e_convento_da_Gra%C3%A7a_I.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Penafiel Palace","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":38.710861,"lo":-9.134389,"d":996,"ds":"The Palace of the Counts of Penafiel (Portuguese: Palácio dos Condes de Penafiel), commonly known as Penafiel Palace (Palácio Penafiel) and also referred as Palace of the Postmaster-General (Palácio do Correio-Mor), is a palace in Portugal, located in the Santa Maria Maior parish, Lisbon.","wpu":"Penafiel Palace","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/Palacio_Conde_de_Penafiel_%28nova_sede_da_CPLP%29.jpg/960px-Palacio_Conde_de_Penafiel_%28nova_sede_da_CPLP%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Praça Martim Moniz","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":38.715472,"lo":-9.136583,"d":1240,"ds":"Praça Martim Moniz is a square in Lisbon, Portugal. It is named after Martim Moniz. There are stops on the Lisbon Metro \"Martim Moniz\" and Lisbon tramway at the square.","wpu":"Praça Martim Moniz","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8b/Pra%C3%A7a_de_Martim_Moniz_-_panoramio.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Statue of José I of Portugal","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":38.707509,"lo":-9.136462,"d":1270,"ds":"The Statue of José I is an equestrian statue of Joseph I of Portugal located in Praça do Comércio in Lisbon. Created by Joaquim Machado de Castro, it was the first equestrian statue made in Portugal, the first public statue in the country dedicated to a living person, and the first of its dimensions to be cast in a single pour in Portugal and one of the first in the world to be so cast.","wpu":"Statue of José I of Portugal","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/Equestrian_statue_of_King_Jos%C3%A9_I_in_Pra%C3%A7a_do_Com%C3%A9rcio_47.jpg/960px-Equestrian_statue_of_King_Jos%C3%A9_I_in_Pra%C3%A7a_do_Com%C3%A9rcio_47.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Fort Santa Apolónia","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":38.723611,"lo":-9.116944,"d":1393,"ds":"The Fort Santa Apolónia (Forte de Santa Apolónia), also known as Santa Apolónia Bulwark (Baluarte de Santa Apolónia in Portuguese) or Battery of Manique (Bateria do Manique), is located in the parish of Penha de França, municipality and District of Lisbon, in Portugal.","wpu":"Fort Santa Apolónia","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0a/ForteSantaApolonia.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Ribeira Palace","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":38.707281,"lo":-9.138222,"d":1421,"ds":"Ribeira Palace (Portuguese pronunciation: [ʁiˈbɐjɾɐ]; Portuguese: Paço da Ribeira) was the main residence of the Kings of Portugal, in Lisbon, for around 250 years. Its construction was ordered by King Manuel I of Portugal when he found the Royal Alcáçova of São Jorge unsuitable.","wpu":"Ribeira Palace","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Terreiro_do_Pa%C3%A7o_antes_do_Terramoto_de_1755.png/960px-Terreiro_do_Pa%C3%A7o_antes_do_Terramoto_de_1755.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Rossio","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":38.713889,"lo":-9.139444,"d":1442,"ds":"The King Pedro IV Square (Portuguese: Praça de D. Pedro IV), popularly known as Rossio ([ʁuˈsi.u]), is a square in the Pombaline Downtown of Lisbon, Portugal. It has been one of its main squares since the Middle Ages. It has been the setting of popular revolts and celebrations, bullfights and executions, and is now a preferred meeting place of Lisbon natives and tourists alike.","wpu":"Rossio","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/52/Pra%C3%A7a_de_D._Pedro_IV.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Carmo Convent","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":38.712222,"lo":-9.14,"d":1475,"ds":"The Convent of Our Lady of Mount Carmel (Portuguese: Convento da Ordem do Carmo) is a former Catholic convent located in the civil parish of Santa Maria Maior, municipality of Lisbon, Portugal.","wpu":"Carmo Convent","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Lisbon09.jpg/960px-Lisbon09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Convent of the Incarnation (Lisbon)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":38.716444,"lo":-9.139083,"d":1480,"ds":"The Convent of the Incarnation (Portuguese: Convento da Encarnação), also known as the Monastery of the Incarnation of the Commandery of St. Benedict of Avis (Portuguese: Mosteiro da Encarnação das Comendadeiras de São Bento de Avis), is a former convent, constructed in 1630, and located in the parish of Arroios, Lisboa, Portugal. Since 1996, it has been classified as a “Property of Public Interest” in Portugal.","wpu":"Convent of the Incarnation (Lisbon)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Convento_da_Encarna%C3%A7%C3%A3o_-_LIsboa%2C_Portugal_01.jpg/960px-Convento_da_Encarna%C3%A7%C3%A3o_-_LIsboa%2C_Portugal_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Estaus Palace","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":38.7148,"lo":-9.1398,"d":1490,"ds":"The Estaus Palace (Portuguese: Paço dos Estaus; Palácio dos Estaus) in Rossio Square, in Lisbon, was the headquarters of the Portuguese Inquisition. The original palace was built on the north side of the square around 1450 as lodging for foreign dignitaries and noblemen visiting Lisbon.","wpu":"Estaus Palace","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/67/Rossio-CloseUp-1598.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Livraria Bertrand","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":38.710639,"lo":-9.141139,"d":1581,"ds":"The Bertrand Library is a Portuguese book retailer operating 59 branches nationwide, including 2 in Madeira. Founded in 1732, its original store in the Chiado neighborhood of Lisbon was declared to be the oldest operating bookstore in the world by the Guinness Book of World Records in 2011.","wpu":"Livraria Bertrand","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/LivrariaBertrand2.JPG/960px-LivrariaBertrand2.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Madre de Deus Convent","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":38.7247,"lo":-9.1141,"d":1609,"ds":"The Convento de Madre de Deus, or Madre de Deus Convent, is a former convent and church in Lisbon, Portugal, which houses the National Museum of the Azulejo. It is classified as a National Monument.","wpu":"Madre de Deus Convent","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Igreja_do_Convento_da_Madre_de_Deus%2C_Lisboa_%28Portugal%29.jpg/960px-Igreja_do_Convento_da_Madre_de_Deus%2C_Lisboa_%28Portugal%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"}],"about":{"ds":"Lisbon ( LIZ-bən; Portuguese: Lisboa [liʒˈβoɐ] ) is the capital and most populous city of Portugal, with an estimated population of 658,236 as of 2025, within its administrative limits and 3,353,000 within the metropolis, as of 2025. The city lies in the western part of the Iberian Peninsula, fronting the northern shore of the River Tagus. Lisbon is one of the oldest cities in the world and the second-oldest European capital city (after Athens), predating other modern European capitals by centuries.","wpu":"Lisbon","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f2/Lisboa_-_Portugal_%2852597836992%29.jpg/960px-Lisboa_-_Portugal_%2852597836992%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},"wk":"2026-10-18"}